1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,263
Imagine you put a camera on a tripod in your garden and point it at one patch of sky.

2
00:00:05,263 --> 00:00:08,688
Every clear night, you take a picture of that same patch.

3
00:00:08,688 --> 00:00:12,300
After a week, you have a stack of pictures of the same stars.

4
00:00:13,225 --> 00:00:15,875
Flip through them, and almost nothing changes.

5
00:00:15,875 --> 00:00:24,937
But now and then, something in the sky really does change: a star gets brighter or fainter, a new point of light appears where there was nothing, or something moves.

6
00:00:24,937 --> 00:00:28,088
A flare, an exploding star, an asteroid.

7
00:00:28,088 --> 00:00:31,813
These real changes in the sky are what astronomers are looking for.

8
00:00:31,813 --> 00:00:36,575
That's why survey telescopes photograph large parts of the sky every few nights.

9
00:00:37,450 --> 00:00:40,487
To a computer, each picture is a grid of numbers.

10
00:00:40,487 --> 00:00:45,263
Each pixel value counts the light that landed on that pixel during the exposure time.

11
00:00:45,263 --> 00:00:48,288
A star is a source: a bump in those counts.

12
00:00:48,288 --> 00:00:53,538
Around it, every pixel still collects some light from the sky itself: that's the background.

13
00:00:53,538 --> 00:01:00,387
Even two pixels of plain sky don't read exactly the same number, because light arrives as random photons.

14
00:01:00,387 --> 00:01:01,675
That's noise.

15
00:01:02,525 --> 00:01:06,913
But your pictures also differ for a second reason: they weren't all taken the same way.

16
00:01:06,913 --> 00:01:14,925
In this course, we call that the observing conditions: not just the sky and the air, but also where the camera points and how long it collects light.

17
00:01:14,925 --> 00:01:17,162
The camera may point a little differently.

18
00:01:17,162 --> 00:01:20,350
The moon or a thin haze can make the background brighter.

19
00:01:20,350 --> 00:01:22,475
You might change the exposure time.

20
00:01:22,475 --> 00:01:28,700
And starlight crosses kilometres of moving air, which spreads each star's light over more pixels.

21
00:01:28,700 --> 00:01:32,687
Astronomers call this seeing, and it changes from night to night.

22
00:01:32,687 --> 00:01:47,000
The shape that one point of light takes in the image, set by the telescope and the seeing, is called the point-spread function, or P S F. So two pictures can differ because the sky really changed, or because the observing conditions did.

23
00:01:47,850 --> 00:01:51,000
The picture you compare against is the reference image.

24
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:53,113
It's usually the earlier one.

25
00:01:53,113 --> 00:01:57,075
The new picture, where you look for changes, is the target image.

26
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:00,537
Now, here's what you'll do in this first module.

27
00:02:00,537 --> 00:02:06,113
You'll work with two small, made-up pictures of the same sky, one reference and one target.

28
00:02:06,113 --> 00:02:08,763
You'll start by looking at what a picture is made of.

29
00:02:08,763 --> 00:02:15,038
Then you'll find every way the two pictures differ, and work out a plan to keep only the real changes in the sky.

30
00:02:15,038 --> 00:02:18,987
You'll finish by meeting the cuPhoton stages that carry out your plan.

31
00:02:18,987 --> 00:02:23,200
You won't write any code, and there's no real telescope data yet.

32
00:02:23,200 --> 00:02:27,700
Your tutor is an AI agent: it does the typing, and you do the thinking.
